Keep libstdc++'s __try/__catch pair consistent with the mingw SEH override.

The mingw toolchain patch (0002) installs an override in build_config.h
that maps SEH constructs to C++ exceptions on mingw:

  #if defined(OS_WIN) && defined(COMPILER_GCC)
  #undef __try
  #define __try try
  #undef __except
  #define __except(x) catch(...)
  #endif

This is fine for chromium code that writes `__try { ... } __except(...) { ... }`
but it also clobbers libstdc++'s own __try macro. libstdc++ pairs __try
with __catch (not __except); under -fno-exceptions exception_defines.h
defines them as `if (true)` / `if (false)`. After the chromium override
the pair becomes `try { ... } if (false) { ... }`, which is not a valid
C++ try-block.

GCC's -fno-exceptions accepts that mismatch with only the
-Wtemplate-body warning (suppressed by chromium's precompile.h).
Clang treats it as an error: the parser sees `try { ... }`, looks for
the matching `catch`, finds `if (false)` instead and emits

  bits/forward_list.tcc:167: error: expected catch

This fires from every Qt-side TU that pulls in perfetto (which
includes <forward_list>) -- and once perfetto is in the include chain
it cascades into every libstdc++ template body that uses __try/__catch.

Extend the chromium override so the libstdc++ pair is consistent too:
map __catch to catch(X) and __throw_exception_again to throw. Real
try/catch inside a libstdc++ system header is accepted by clang under
-fno-exceptions (only user-code try blocks are rejected), and the
templates remain dead code paths after dead-store elimination just as
they did with the `if (false)` form -- no runtime exception machinery
gets pulled in.

--- a/src/3rdparty/chromium/build/build_config.h
+++ b/src/3rdparty/chromium/build/build_config.h
@@ -439,6 +439,13 @@
 #define __try try
 #undef __except
 #define __except(x) catch(...)
+// libstdc++ pairs __try with __catch / __throw_exception_again (not
+// __except). Keep its expansion consistent with the override above so
+// template bodies parse as `try { ... } catch(X) { ... }`.
+#undef __catch
+#define __catch(X) catch(X)
+#undef __throw_exception_again
+#define __throw_exception_again throw
 #endif
 
 #endif  // BUILD_BUILD_CONFIG_H_
